 add a preposition before this Sunday."}}},{"id":"0913fb60-a556-551f-adcd-4e4eb541602c","topicSlug":"prepositions-of-time","level":"A1","prompt":"The last bus leaves ____ this Sunday.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"in"},{"id":"B","text":"—"},{"id":"C","text":"at"},{"id":"D","text":"on"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"Use at with clock times and expressions such as noon, midnight and dawn. Focus on **zero** in this question.","explanation":"Do not add a preposition before this Sunday.","keywords":[],"source":"seed","skill":"zero","contextKey":"time-50-2","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-time","pcc-esol"],"version":"foundations-2026-09-v1","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"fingerprint":"65caa9a53a886044cac1b4a8d4cc84f55da9ee95e1b06f80f3a529bf99239b15","teaching":{"steps":["Identify what the question tests: **zero**. Use at with clock times and expressions such as noon, midnight and dawn.","Do not add a preposition before this Sunday.","Choose **—**. Check the completed expression against the subject, time and intended meaning in the prompt."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"In normally marks larger periods or parts of the day, not this time phrase. Do not add a preposition before this Sunday.","B":"Do not add a preposition before this Sunday.","C":"At normally marks clock times and certain fixed expressions, not this time phrase. Do not add a preposition before this Sunday.","D":"On normally marks days and dates, not this time phrase. Do not add a preposition before this Sunday."}}},{"id":"1b604f48-f132-52d4-a6d9-2a10dbb3403f","topicSlug":"prepositions-of-time","level":"A1","prompt":"The library opens ____ this Sunday.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"at"},{"id":"B","text":"on"},{"id":"C","text":"in"},{"id":"D","text":"—"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"Use at with clock times and expressions such as noon, midnight and dawn. Focus on **zero** in this question.","explanation":"Do not add a preposition before this Sunday.","keywords":[],"source":"seed","skill":"zero","contextKey":"time-50-3","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-time","pcc-esol"],"version":"foundations-2026-09-v1","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"fingerprint":"70cb142921a656f0425ffc54098e49abd40927eaadd3bcde8f2d64c983dae890","teaching":{"steps":["Identify what the question tests: **zero**. Use at with clock times and expressions such as noon, midnight and dawn.","Do not add a preposition before this Sunday.","Choose **—**. Check the completed expression against the subject, time and intended meaning in the prompt."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"At normally marks clock times and certain fixed expressions, not this time phrase. Do not add a preposition before this Sunday.","B":"On normally marks days and dates, not this time phrase. Do not add a preposition before this Sunday.","C":"In normally marks larger periods or parts of the day, not this time phrase. Do not add a preposition before this Sunday.","D":"Do not add a preposition before this Sunday."}}},{"id":"39ec9fc7-fa67-5bd0-a50c-bb5fa6957b22","topicSlug":"prepositions-of-time","level":"A1","prompt":"The delivery is due ____ this Sunday.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"in"},{"id":"B","text":"—"},{"id":"C","text":"at"},{"id":"D","text":"on"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"Use at with clock times and expressions such as noon, midnight and dawn. Focus on **zero** in this question.","explanation":"Do not add a preposition before this Sunday.","keywords":[],"source":"seed","skill":"zero","contextKey":"time-50-4","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-time","pcc-esol"],"version":"foundations-2026-09-v1","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"fingerprint":"d78aa70f46d28e27036ea82999a97e5c5f86b19f19418e0b63ed4d00c42058be","teaching":{"steps":["Identify what the question tests: **zero**. Use at with clock times and expressions such as noon, midnight and dawn.","Do not add a preposition before this Sunday.","Choose **—**. Check the completed expression against the subject, time and intended meaning in the prompt."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"In normally marks larger periods or parts of the day, not this time phrase. Do not add a preposition before this Sunday.","B":"Do not add a preposition before this Sunday.","C":"At normally marks clock times and certain fixed expressions, not this time phrase. Do not add a preposition before this Sunday.","D":"On normally marks days and dates, not this time phrase. Do not add a preposition before this Sunday."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["Time prepositions group expressions by how English conventionally presents them: as a point, a day/date or a larger period. These are useful patterns rather than rules based only on duration. Some expressions take no preposition at all."],"rules":[{"title":"At","explanation":"Use at with clock times and expressions such as noon, midnight and dawn.","examples":["The gates open at 8:15.","We leave at sunrise.","We meet **at** noon **on** Friday."],"pattern":"**at** six · **at** night","paragraphs":["At commonly introduces clock times and certain fixed time expressions such as at night and at the moment. A festival period may take at in British English: at Christmas. When naming the particular day, use on Christmas Day."]},{"title":"On and in","explanation":"Use on with days and dates, including a named day plus a part of that day. Use in with months and with the morning, afternoon or evening.","examples":["on Thursday afternoon","in November","She was born **in** April **in** 2001."],"pattern":"**on** Monday · **on** 12 May · **on** Friday morning","paragraphs":["On introduces days and dates. Adding a day to a part of the day usually changes in to on: in the morning but on Tuesday morning. English names of days and months begin with capitals."]},{"title":"No preposition","explanation":"Usually omit a time preposition before next, last, this or every, and before today, tomorrow and yesterday. Here, — means leave the gap empty.","examples":["The rehearsal is next week.","I will call tomorrow.","I’ll see you **next Monday**, not on next Monday."],"pattern":"**in** June · **in** 2030 · **in** the morning","paragraphs":["In introduces months, years, seasons and many parts of the day. It can also mean after a period from now: in ten minutes. For gives a duration and since gives a starting point; they answer different time questions."]},{"title":"Contrasts and edge cases","explanation":"Next, last, this and every usually come without in/on/at: next week, last year, this morning, every Friday. British at the weekend and American on the weekend are both standard patterns. By marks a deadline; until marks continuation up to a point.","examples":["We meet **at** noon **on** Friday.","She was born **in** April **in** 2001.","I’ll see you **next Monday**, not on next Monday."]}],"recap":["**at** six · **at** night","**on** Monday · **on** 12 May · **on** Friday morning","**in** June · **in** 2030 · **in** the morning"]},"stage":"2 · Everyday descriptions","prerequisites":["determiners-quantifiers"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
